0..78 Comment "// An atom named inside the abstraction that binds it, read by its depth below"
79..93 Comment "// its binder."
94..96 Comment "//"
97..172 Comment "// Admission re-mints an atom with its binder, so a rule naming the channel"
173..249 Comment "// its restriction abstracts is one rule whatever the atom is called. A rule"
250..326 Comment "// stating a freshness condition fires only where its instance names no atom"
327..341 Comment "// it may not."
342..344 Comment "//"
345..423 Comment "// Matching, the critical pairs and the kernel\'s replay count the abstractions"
424..501 Comment "// between an occurrence and its binder. A rule\'s variable is a hole over the"
502..578 Comment "// atoms it may name, so the check decides an overlap under the condition it"
579..648 Comment "// composes. Each theory below normalizes one process its rules meet."
649..651 Comment "//"
652..729 Comment "// - see: #1934 — not implemented: a rule matching a variable both under an"
730..760 Comment "//   abstraction and beside it"
761..835 Comment "// - see: `pathological/desc/atom-occurrence.gandr` — that rule, refused"
836..904 Comment "// - see: Fernández and Gabbay, _Nominal rewriting_, I&C 2007, § 2"
905..952 Comment "// - see: Pitts, _Nominal Sets_, CUP 2013, § 4"
953..1032 Comment "// - see: Milner, _Communicating and Mobile Systems: the π-Calculus_, CUP 1999"
1033..1099 Comment "// - see: Mayr and Nipkow, _Higher-order rewrite systems and their"
1100..1126 Comment "//   confluence_, TCS 1998"
1127..1199 Comment "// - see: Miller, _A logic programming language with lambda-abstraction,"
1200..1258 Comment "//   function variables, and simple unification_, JLC 1991"
1260..1336 Comment "/// A send on a restricted channel, which no process outside the restriction"
1337..1414 Comment "/// can receive: the process never moves, so it is the inert one. The checker"
1415..1491 Comment "/// orients the law and the kernel replays the verdict; a restriction naming"
1492..1551 Comment "/// its channel `b` normalizes by the law written over `a`."
1552..1554 Other "@["
1555..1565 Other "normalizes"
1565..1566 Other "("
1566..1567 StringLit "\""
1567..1598 StringLit "nu(bind b . out(b, nil)) ~> nil"
